    "result_text": "The full suite runs (there's only one test class, `AcceptanceTest`; the other files are the abstract base and a helper) and everything compiles and passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the per-city owner cap by following the codebase's existing validation conventions rather than inlining the check.\n\n**New files:**\n- `CityOwnerCapacityValidator` \u2014 a `@Component` that reuses the existing `CityOwnerCounter` and throws when a city already holds `MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY` (50) or more owners. Single responsibility: capacity enforcement.\n- `CityOwnerCapacityExceededException` \u2014 a `RuntimeException` mirroring the existing `DuplicateOwnerHousehold`/`DuplicateOwnerTelephone` exceptions.\n\n**Edits:**\n- `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` \u2014 injected the validator and call `validateHasCapacity(owner.getCity())` alongside the other uniqueness checks, before persisting.\n- `ExceptionControllerAdvice` \u2014 added a handler mapping `CityOwnerCapacityExceededException` to **409 Conflict** with an `errors: [\"city\"]` body, matching the shape of the sibling 409 handlers.\n\nThe logic reuses `CityOwnerCounter` (whose count is case-insensitive) instead of duplicating the owner-scanning logic, and each new class has a single, focused responsibility. The `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity` (fill a city to 50, expect the 51st to be rejected) passes, and the full build is green.",
